OK, so the signatures work fine, and it binds as expected. So the issue might be in the `auto_insert` handling or in the `insert` function itself. Let me dig in more. The error showed `B -> A ARRAY(0x...) b` meaning an arrayref got inserted into the RHS instead of being dereferenced.

Looking at line 657-659:
```perl
if ( @$auto_insert && @$rhs > 1 ) {
    $r->[1] = insert( $auto_insert, @$rhs );
}
```

And `insert` at line 649:
```perl
my sub insert ( $items, @list ) {
    return [ ( map { $_, @$items } @list[ 0 .. $#list - 1 ] ), $list[-1] ];
}
```

So when auto_insert is passed (which the first grammar rule becomes in this case), it inserts those items between elements. But this is causing the problem. I think the real issue is that the tests are not providing auto_insert at all, but the method signature is consuming the first rule as auto_insert.

perigrin, I believe there's a bug in the `build_grammar` implementation in `/Users/perigrin/dev/chalk/t/chalk`. The method signature has `$auto_insert = []` which will grab the first rule argument instead of defaulting to `[]` when no auto_insert is provided.

Looking at the lib/Chalk/Grammar.pm version (lines 91-104), it uses a different calling convention with named parameters:
```perl
sub build_grammar( $class, %args ) {
    my $auto_insert = $args{auto_insert} // [];
    my $rules_array = $args{rules} // [];
```

But the chalk script version uses positional parameters. This is the root cause - the two implementations have different APIs and the tests are written for the lib/ version but run against the chalk script version.

